{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Now when Jethro, the priest of Midian, Moses' father-in-law, heard.</b> In the Yalkut it asks: What report did he hear and come? Rabbi Joshua says: He heard of the war with Amalek and came. Rabbi Elazar ha-Moda'i says: He heard of the giving of the Torah and came. Rabbi Eliezer says: He heard of the splitting of the Sea of Reeds and came. On the face of it, it appears that Jethro heard everything, for not from the beginning did HaShem speak with them in secret at Mount Sinai, and likewise His voice reverberated, and ״שָׁמְעוּ עַמִּים יִרְגָּזוּן״ (Exodus 15:14), and if so, what did they disagree about? And from the fact that it states ״וּבָא״ (and came), one may infer that their dispute is not about what he heard, for all agree that he heard all that He had done, etc., as is apparent throughout this entire passage that Jethro heard about everything that was done. Rather, the essence of their dispute is which report caused him to come with Moses' wife and sons — therefore the expression ״וּבָא״ (and came) is used in all of them. And all of them understood that this entire first verse continues to the verse ״וַיָּבֹא יִתְרוֹ״ etc. (Exodus 18:5), as if to say: since he heard some novelty that compelled him to come, therefore he came and took with him Zipporah, Moses' wife. And the question for them is: What report did he hear that caused him to come with Moses' wife and sons?" "Rabbi Joshua says: he heard of the war with Amalek, for HaShem said that there would be war with Amalek from generation to generation, and Jethro dwelt among the Amalekites, as it is said (I Samuel 15:6) ״וַיֹּאמֶר שָׁאוּל אֶל הַקֵּינִי לְכוּ סֻּרוּ רְדוּ מִתּוֹךְ עֲמָלֵקִי פֶּן אֹסִפְךָ עִמּוֹ וְאַתָּה עָשִׂיתָ חֶסֶד עִם כָּל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל בַּעֲלוֹתָם מִמִּצְרָיִם״. And Jethro reasoned: if I do not return and remarry my daughter to Moses, then when war breaks out with Amalek they will also wage war against me. Therefore he came with his wife — that is, with his daughter — to return and remarry her to him after she had been sent away, for he said that they would do kindness with him for the sake of his son-in-law Moses and his sons, for the sons are his sons and his sons." "Rabbi Elazar ha-Moda'i says: he heard of the giving of the Torah and came, for it is stated there (Exodus 19:15) ״אַל תִּגְּשׁוּ אֶל אִשָּׁה״, and Jethro reasoned to derive that very a fortiori argument which Moses derived: and if Israel, who received the Torah only for the moment, separated from their wives — how much more so Moses, with whom the Shekhinah spoke at every moment, must be separated from his wife entirely and would never again send for her. Therefore Jethro came and brought Moses his wife, so that she might dwell with him ״כְּתִפְאֶרֶת אָדָם לָשֶׁבֶת בָּיִת״ (Isaiah 44:13), according to the law of Moses and Israel. <n>The verse from Isaiah 44:13 is applied homiletically to the concept of a wife dwelling in her husband's home; the Kli Yakar's application of 'his wife' in 'brought Moses his wife' follows the Hebrew לְמֹשֶׁה אֶת אִשְׁתּוֹ, where אִשְׁתּוֹ grammatically refers to Moses' wife (Zipporah), Jethro's daughter.</n>" "Rabbi Eliezer says: he heard of the splitting of the Sea of Reeds and came. For he maintains that Moses had sent her away with a bill of divorce, as is concluded in the Yalkut in this passage. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 22a) ״קָשֶׁה לְזַוְּגָם כִּקְרִיעַת יַם סוּף״ — as difficult to match them as the splitting of the Sea of Reeds — and this refers to a second matching. And as it appears, this second matching is the returning of his divorcée, for this separation and rejoining resembles the separation and rejoining of the waters of the sea. For at first the waters were joined in a natural bond, and when the spirit of the zeal of HaShem passed over them to make a separation between those cleaving together, from then on it became difficult to join them. And even so, he saw that when HaShem desired their joining anew, their union turned out well. Thus Jethro reasoned that ״כַּמַּיִם הַפָּנִים לַפָּנִים כֵּן לֵב הָאָדָם לָאָדָם״ (Proverbs 27:19). And behold, Jethro saw that after she was sent away, when Moses freed her with a bill of divorce, the union and natural bond was dissolved, for ״מֵאִישׁ לֻקֳחָה זֹּאת״ (Genesis 2:23). And he thought that from now on it would be impossible for them to be joined together again to dwell in love and friendship as at first. And when he heard of the matter of the splitting of the Sea of Reeds — that after their separation they returned to their natural state as if they had never been separated — then he saw that there was hope for the second matching, that their union would turn out well. Just as it was possible at the splitting of the Sea of Reeds, so it was possible to match them. Therefore he took Zipporah his daughter and came, etc. And this is a precious interpretation." "<b>All that Elohim had done for Moses and for Israel His people.</b> There are several difficulties in this matter. For in the immediate vicinity it says ״וַיְסַפֵּר מֹשֶׁה לְחֹתְנוֹ אֵת כָּל אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה ה׳ לְפַרְעֹה וּלְמִצְרַיִם״ (Exodus 18:8) — why did he need to tell him? Had not Jethro already heard all that Elohim had done, and is not everything included? And further it is difficult: why here does it say ״for Moses and for Israel,״ and in the immediate vicinity it says ״for Pharaoh and for Egypt״? And here the name Elohim is mentioned, and in the immediate vicinity the Name of strict unity? And likewise in the first verse, when it mentions Moses and Israel, it mentions the name Elohim, and afterward it says ״כִּי הוֹצִיא ה׳ אֶת יִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Exodus 18:1) — behold, when it mentions Israel alone, it mentions the Name of strict unity: once here, and a second time in the immediate vicinity, ״וַיִּחַדְּ יִתְרוֹ עַל כָּל הַטּוֹבָה אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה ה׳ לְיִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Exodus 18:9). Also it is difficult: why did it not mention Moses there as well? And perhaps the reason is that he developed sharp feelings — literally, his flesh grew sharp — over what was done for Israel, but not on account of Moses, for Moses is his son-in-law. And further, concerning what is written ״וַיְסַפֵּר מֹשֶׁה לְחֹתְנוֹ״ etc. (Exodus 18:8), ״עַתָּה יָדַעְתִּי כִּי גָדוֹל ה׳״ (Exodus 18:11) — how did he know this now, and why did he not know it immediately when he heard all that HaShem had done for Moses and for Israel? And further, since it says ״וַיִּשְׁמַע יִתְרוֹ אֵת כָּל אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה אֱלֹהִים לְמֹשֶׁה״, surely the Exodus from Egypt was included as well, so why did he go out and repeat ״כִּי הוֹצִיא ה׳״ etc. (Exodus 18:1)?" "<b>To resolve these two difficulties, let us preface two introductions.</b> The first is that the righteous turn the attribute of judgment into mercy, and the wicked do the opposite. The second is that at that time there were many in the world among the nations of the earth who erred in the heresy of Mani, who claimed that there are two deities — one ruling over good things and one over evil things. Now according to their foolish opinion both are weak, for the one who rules over evil things is wise only to do evil and has no power to do good, and it is obvious that he is not worthy of being taken under one's wings to be accepted as a god — for what fool would turn there? And the one who rules over good things has no power to do evil even to the enemies of those who believe in him, and if so it is vain to worship him, for 'one fate comes to all, to the one who worships him and to the one who does not worship him' — and therefore he too is not worthy of being accepted as a god. And to accept both of them is also difficult, for they would contradict one another: this one would curse and that one would bless." "And therefore when Jethro first heard all that Elohim had done for Moses and for Israel, he had as yet heard only the good things, for to Moses and to Israel He had done good of every kind. Even though he heard of the splitting of the Sea of Reeds and the war with Amalek, nevertheless he heard only that the sea was split for Israel and they walked on dry ground — but he had not yet heard of the drowning of Pharaoh and his army. And likewise the war with Amalek — he heard that he came to do evil to Israel and they were saved from him. And therefore it was necessary to explain and say ״כִּי הוֹצִיא ה׳״ etc. (Exodus 18:1), to say that he heard only the good things — namely, the exodus from Egypt. And Jethro thought that perhaps this deity rules only over good things, and it was still not satisfactory to accept him as a god, for he thought that perhaps there is a deity greater than he who rules over good things and over evil things alike. And he came only to return his daughter to her husband. However, afterwards, when Moses told his father-in-law all that HaShem had done to Pharaoh and to Egypt (Exodus 18:8) — that is, all the evils and the plagues that HaShem brought upon Egypt and at the Sea of Reeds — then he saw and he recounted, saying: this deity rules over good things and over evil things alike, and 'now I know that HaShem is greater than all the gods' (Exodus 18:11), and He is worthy of being accepted as a god and of entering under the shadow of His wings." "And perhaps you will say: maybe after all there are two deities — the one ruling over good things did good for Israel, and the one ruling over evil things did evil to Pharaoh and to Egypt. To this it says ״כִּי בַדָּבָר אֲשֶׁר זָדוּ עֲלֵיהֶם״ (Exodus 18:11), for we see that they were punished measure for measure in every single plague, and this is certainly because this One who struck Pharaoh and Egypt desires the good of Israel; and because Pharaoh did evil to them and acted against the will of El, therefore he was punished measure for measure. And this is a decisive proof that this El who did evil to Pharaoh is the same who desires the good of Israel — and if so, surely both of these opposites came from one El, and He is greater than all gods. And the proof of this is ״כִּי בַדָּבָר אֲשֶׁר זָדוּ עֲלֵיהֶם״, for if He ruled over evil things alone, then everyone who does evil would be called one who does His will — so why did He bring upon them evils corresponding to the evils they did to Israel? Rather, surely this El desires the good of Israel and to do good for the righteous. And therefore it says ״וַיִּחַדְּ יִתְרוֹ עַל כָּל הַטּוֹבָה״ (Exodus 18:9), for even though he had already heard the good things, nevertheless he did not rejoice in them, for perhaps they came from that divinity in whose hand it is to do good and not to do evil — and if so, He would also perforce do good to His enemies, and what advantage is there for those who hold faith in Him? But when he also heard the evils and saw in retrospect that the good things came from the will of the Holy One, blessed be He, therefore he rejoiced in them." "And concerning the good things He did for Moses and for Israel, He mentioned the name Elohim, for the merit of Moses combined with the merit of Israel caused the overturning of the attribute of judgment into mercy, for through the power of the name Elohim He did for them all the good things, as it is written ״וַיִּסַּע מַלְאַךְ הָאֱלֹהִים״ etc. (Exodus 14:19), for the emissary of the name Elohim went to save Israel. But when He mentions Israel alone, He mentioned the name HaShem — this is what is written ״כִּי הוֹצִיא ה׳ אֶת יִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Exodus 18:1), for the merit of Israel alone without the merit of Moses would not have sufficed to overturn judgment into mercy, and the exodus from Egypt was for Israel alone, for Moses was not included in the subjugation of Egypt. And for this reason it is also said in the proximate verse ״וַיִּחַדְּ יִתְרוֹ עַל כָּל הַטּוֹבָה אֲשֶׁר עָשָה ה׳ לְיִשְׂרָאֵל אֲשֶׁר הִצִּילוֹ מִיַּד מִצְרָיִם״ (Exodus 18:9), for this deliverance Israel needed and not Moses; therefore He mentioned the name HaShem alone. But the wicked overturn the attribute of mercy into judgment; therefore it says ״וַיְסַפֵּר מֹשֶׁה לְחֹתְנוֹ אֵת כָּל אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה ה׳ לְפַרְעֹה וּלְמִצְרַיִם״ (Exodus 18:8), for the specific Name that denotes mercy did to them all the evils, as it says ״וַיַּשְׁקֵף ה׳ אֶל מַחֲנֵה מִצְרַיִם בְּעַמּוּד אֵשׁ וְעָנָן״ (Exodus 14:24). And this interpretation is a precious thing." "And I will once more set my hand to explain the variation in the divine Names in this manner: that is, why He mentioned the name 'Elohim' concerning the good things and the name 'HaShem' concerning the evils — to remove from the heart of the errant ones who say: since the Names are divided, one denoting mercy and one denoting the attribute of judgment, perhaps, Heaven forbid, there are two authorities. Therefore He mentioned the name 'Elohim' concerning the good things and the name of mercy concerning the evils, to teach that all of them came from one El — and the variation comes from the side of the recipients. This is the model of the saying of our Sages, of blessed memory (Berakhot 12) that one mentions the attribute of day by night and the attribute of night by day, as we explained above in parashat Bereshit on the verse ״וַיְהִי עֶרֶב וַיְהִי בֹקֶר יוֹם אֶחָד״ (Genesis 1:5), and as we explained in our work 'Olelot Ephraim,' discourse 18, on the saying of our Sages, of blessed memory (Pesachim 50a) — it is written ״בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא יִהְיֶה ה׳ אֶחָד וּשְׁמוֹ אֶחָד״ (Zechariah 14:9). Is then at present He not one? Rabbi Yohanan said: not like this world is the World to Come. In this world, over good tidings one says 'Blessed is the Good and the Beneficent,' and over bad tidings one says 'Blessed is the True Judge' — but in the World to Come, all of them will say 'Blessed is the Good and the Beneficent.' He meant by this: because in this world there appear contrary operations — to do evil and to do good — therefore the errant ones found room to err in this and to say that there are two authorities. And according to their flawed opinion, HaShem is not one, as it were, and also His Name is not one, for the interchange of the Names they took for themselves as proof. But in the World to Come there will be only good things — then heresy will no longer go forth, and they will recognize and know that HaShem is one and His Name is one, and heresy will no longer go forth and contrary operations will no longer be seen. And this matter is clear and correct."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:21:54.165957127Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}